<h1>Comparing the two approaches</h1>

<p>Short answer: prefer the event-driven version. It does less work, fails more loudly, and
reads the way the data actually flows.</p>

<h2>Why polling loses</h2>

<p>Polling looks simple until you price it. Every interval is a request whether anything
changed or not, and the interval itself is a compromise: too short wastes calls, too long
shows stale state. You end up tuning a number that has no right value.</p>

<ul>
    <li>Steady request load even when nothing changes.</li>
    <li>Latency is the interval on average, twice the interval at worst.</li>
    <li>Backoff, dedup and retry logic all live in your code.</li>
</ul>

<h2>The event-driven version</h2>

<p>Subscribe once, react when something actually happens, and let the connection carry the
quiet periods for free:</p>

<pre><code>const stream = client.subscribe("orders");

stream.on("update", (order) => {
  render(order);
});</code></pre>

<p>The trade-offs are real but bounded:</p>

<table>
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th>Concern</th>
            <th>Polling</th>
            <th>Events</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td>Latency</td>
            <td>Interval-bound</td>
            <td>Immediate</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td>Idle cost</td>
            <td>Constant</td>
            <td>Near zero</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td>Reconnect logic</td>
            <td>None needed</td>
            <td>Required</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<blockquote>
    <p>Rule of thumb: poll when you own the interval, subscribe when the world does.</p>
</blockquote>

<p>Want the reconnect-with-resume pattern next? It is about twenty lines and removes the last
real objection to the event-driven approach.</p>
